  "worked_solution": "To express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{235}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${235}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275599/assets/c9b63de223f4633b1c663ddde52b9a3ccc62ab8031528c117ecdc8766d94dc7b.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={235}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={235}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={55}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{235}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{55}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{235}^{∘}$ must be *positive* because the tangent ratio is *always* positive in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{235}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{55}^{∘}.\n$$",
